{"name":"Watermelon Crush","hex":"#bf6c6e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"watermelon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the species Citrullus lanatus, bearing a melon-like fruit.","The fruit of the watermelon plant, having a green rind and watery flesh that is typically bright red when ripe and contains black seeds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/watermelon"},{"term":"crush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent collision or compression; a crash; destruction; ruin.","Violent pressure, as of a moving crowd."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To press between two hard objects; to squeeze so as to alter the natural shape or integrity, or to force together into a mass.","To reduce to fine particles by pounding or grinding."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crush (drink)","blocks":[{"text":"Crush is a brand of carbonated soft drinks originally created as an orange soda, Orange-Crush. The brand is owned and marketed internationally by Keurig Dr Pepper, but is marketed by PepsiCo in North America. It was created in 1911, 29 years before The Coca-Cola Company's Fanta, by beverage and extract chemist Neil C. Ward. Most flavors of Crush are caffeine-free."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Crush_Soda_Logo_New.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crush_(drink)"},{"title":"Watermelon","blocks":[{"text":"The watermelon is a species of flowering plant in the family Cucurbitaceae. It is a scrambling and trailing vine-like plant cultivated worldwide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Taiwan_2009_Tainan_City_Organic_Farm_Watermelon_FRD_7962.jpg/330px-Taiwan_2009_Tainan_City_Organic_Farm_Watermelon_FRD_7962.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org